problems.problem:
One of the processes that may influence the explosions of some supernovae is the creation of electron–positron pairs from high-energy gamma quanta inside the star. As a result of these reactions the pressure inside the star suddenly drops and the equilibrium between the pressure of gas and radiation and the gravitational forces is disturbed, which causes a collapse, a rise of temperature, the activation of further fusion reactions and, as a consequence, an explosion accompanied by the ejection of a wave of matter;

a) estimate at what temperatures inside the star such a process can occur?

In sufficiently massive stars such explosions may happen several times, the successive ejections of matter being less and less massive but of comparable energy, so that the velocity of the matter in successive waves is greater and greater. When the second wave of matter catches up with the first, a very strong heating of the matter occurs at the contact of the waves and an enormous amount of energy is radiated. Probably such a scenario can explain the behaviour of the brightest supernovae (for example SN 2006gy), for which the classical model is unable to generate the required explosion energy;

b) taking the radiated power at maximum as $L = 2.5\cdot10^{36}$ W and the radius of the sphere where the second wave catches up with the first as $R = 10^{13}$ m, determine the effective temperature of the matter at the contact of these waves.

Note: several answers may be correct in each question. A question is credited if all the correct answers and only the correct answers are indicated.

1.1) Mark the leap years in the Gregorian calendar:
(A) 1746
(B) 1900
(C) 2400
(D) 3200

1.2) On the first day of spring a weary traveller drove a stick vertically into a flat patch of sand and found that the length of the shadow at the moment of true noon was five times smaller than the height of the stick above the sand. Calculate at what geographical latitude the traveller could have been.
(A) 11° N
(B) 11° S
(C) 79° N
(D) 79° S

1.3) What result can a high jumper achieve on the Moon if on the Earth he achieves a result close to his height, i.e. about 2 metres?
(A) 2 m
(B) 4 m
(C) 7 m
(D) 12 m

1.4) At approximately what time will the dust moons located at the Lagrange points L4 and L5 of the Earth–Moon system culminate, if the Moon is at first quarter?
(A) 14
(B) 18
(C) 22
(D) 02

1.5) The Earth's atmosphere transmits electromagnetic radiation of:
(A) wavelength 500 nm;
(B) frequency 10 GHz;
(C) energy 8 keV;
(D) wavelength 500 Å.

1.6) A certain star is at a distance of 100 pc from the Sun and lies on the ecliptic. As a result of heliocentric parallax, during a year its
(A) ecliptic longitude varies within an interval of width 0.01″;
(B) ecliptic longitude varies within an interval of width 0.02″;
(C) ecliptic latitude varies within an interval of width 0.01″;
(D) ecliptic latitude varies within an interval of width 0.02″.

1.7) The maximum of radiated power in the spectrum of a certain star falls at a wavelength two times shorter than the maximum of radiated power in the spectrum of the Sun. This means that approximately the radiated power (luminosity) of this star is:
(A) two times greater than the radiated power of the Sun;
(B) two times smaller than the radiated power of the Sun;
(C) four times greater than the radiated power of the Sun;
(D) sixteen times greater than the radiated power of the Sun.

1.8) Energy transport in a main-sequence star of mass 3 solar masses takes place by means of:
(A) convection only;
(B) radiation only;
(C) convection in the core of the star and radiation in its outer layers;
(D) radiation in the core of the star and convection in its outer layers.

1.9) Two stars, A and B, are in the same star cluster and have the same colour index. Star A is brighter by 2.5 mag than star B. This means that
(A) the radius of star A is 10 times greater than the radius of star B;
(B) the radius of star A is 3.2 times greater than the radius of star B;
(C) the radius of star A is 3.2 times smaller than the radius of star B;
(D) the radius of star A is 10 times smaller than the radius of star B.

1.10) Consider two identical galaxies, A and B, whose redshifts are greater than one ($z > 1$). The angular diameter of galaxy A is two times greater than the angular diameter of galaxy B. This means that galaxy A is brighter than galaxy B by a factor
(A) greater than 4;
(B) exactly 4;
(C) smaller than 4;
(D) the question does not contain enough data to calculate this.
